d mine the  exhibition a number of different ways  you can go back  a number of times and see the works in  conversation with one another based on  very different  vantage points so that was another kind  of solution to some of the challenges  that we  faced and in that same vein the  exhibition itself the layout of the work  is extremely open uh we tried not to  cordon off any particular areas or  create specific groups within our works  going back to this notion of blurring  boundaries there's a lot of space for  the viewer in terms of eye line was  something that we had many discussions  about  in our curatorial process  i want to ask mikhail  how do you as an artist from south  africa feel about  seeing your work contextualized in in  this kind of an exhibition  that focuses on  contemporary work from south africa as  opposed to being in an exhibition of  contemporary art generally or an  exhibition of the entire african  continent or some other subset of  contemporary art what does it mean for  you to be in an exhibition like this  thank you um  before i answer that i do want to  congratulate the curators and yourself  kate um i think it's a very very  coherent and interesting presentation of  contemporary south african art and um  one that i wanted to be a part of so  thank you for that  um  what you're raising is is difficult for  me as a as a young-ish south african  artist  um  uh  i  i've been a part of a number of kind of  survey shows both of south african art  and  african art from across the continent  and you always do get the feeling that  as interesting as the exhibition might  be you are being put in a little bit of  a basket just  in the same way as uh photographic  surveys might do in another respect to  my work  um but i also understand that uh  every exhibition needs  its limitations and sometimes  nationality or  geography is an important starting point  for that  i think  in  in south africa  we have a heavy burden of particular  lines of thought  and  kind of themes within our artistic  production and  i think uh the important thing is to  is to as an artist from an artist's  perspective is that one doesn't get  weighed down by by how heavy those those  things are historically  um themes of identity themes of politics  and demonstration and and so yeah i  appreciate the fact that this exhibition  has not um  uh  gone in in predictable ways on those  lines  thank you  as student curators for many of us it  was our first curating experience and we  came to this idea of blurring boundaries  before we knew how it would manifest in  the final exhibition stage and so to  make all of this real we relied on a lot  of expertise in the rest of the  gallery's community and in the obviously  with kate's mentorship but also from  different departments i think that was  maybe one of the most  exciting aspects of being a student  curator  was the the combination of  bringing  insightful and new and fresh and kind of  uh  perhaps uncuratory ideas  and seeing those manifest with gallery  expertise in the interests of putting  together this exhibition that we're very  proud of  so  i know we're all so pleased with the way  the exhibition looks now and the way  it's it's turned out but i know from  having been part of the weekly meetings  that there were several challenges and  pressure points  so  differences of opinion and lots of  discussion  so i'm wondering what did you find most  challenging about this project  what were some of those issues that you  needed to  discuss and overcome and resolve  so i think to begin with the tech coming  from a technical standpoint uh one of  the most challenging uh dimensions of  this process for me  was not being able to see the work in  person per se we did actually borrow  a great deal from outside of the  institution i think one of the most  helpful  points in retrospect was being able to  see the core works of the of the actual  yale art gallery collection um in person  and to