ry to be as diverse in our selection as  possible i mean on one hand we wanted to  first select based upon the the work  itself and what attracted us most  and that was actually for me something  that i felt was a very valuable learning  experience because i didn't come from  sort of this art history side or even  the fine arts side  so actually i feel like what i've  learned the most is just to fall in love  with art  but then yes of course because of my  particular background  very concerned about issues of you know  colonial gays or the politics of  representation and so um  that definitely came into play after we  had like a certain set of works that we  really loved um and then thinking about  the stories behind each of these artists  because i think  we've come to know these works almost  like people  i think even for us seeing our own show  now and seeing it all up after the  decisions and the fights we had  advocating for certain ones to be in or  in the end i think we really feel that  we know these works  we  we really appreciate the artists  themselves we appreciate their process  the final product  it helps if you know something about  sort of the south african context but  it's not necessary for you to really  appreciate what's there before you um so  i think it really it really operates on  a lot of different levels i wouldn't say  race was like kind of the the sole thing  that made us pick a certain artist it's  certainly part of it  and we have to be sensitive to that but  at the same time we have to balance that  uh  yeah balance sort of the appreciation  for art itself  yeah i think also for me as someone who  teaches from the collections as a  gallery teacher and has done so for the  last four years that's a really  important question that we should ask of  all the work that's in any museum it's  not a question that should be  you know  central just to south africa or  essentially just to contemporary art but  when we're in front of a beer shot when  we're in front of rosco when we're in  front of any number of works that we  teach from how do we  not impose those questions but  sort of bring out the nuances that those  questions  would suggest so that was definitely  a point that we discussed but also  within a larger context of  the museum and the works that other  visitors are going to see before and  after encountering this exhibition  and how can we be a part of that  conversation how can we continue that  conversation and not have it just start  and end on the fourth floor  and in terms of just to add one quick  thing in terms of  the sort of ratios within the show or  the artists themselves we were very much  focused on the works  specifically and how the work spoke to  us  beyond the racial identity of  of the artists who produce them although  quite often that was a big you know  stake within that work of art  hi  hi my name is yuchana and i'm a graduate  student at the university of texas at  austin and i'm really curious about how  your group came together and what the  process was in terms of how you were  selected did you apply and i've heard  mention of three semesters so i'm  wondering how that worked out and  meeting once a week i'm just wondering  about the format of  the process of curating the show like  did you get credit you know all those  kind of details thank you  so i'll just  i'll just start off by um  saying that  when we got the go-ahead to do this show  and we knew when the  the dates would be  i sent out a call for  applications for participation i sent  emails to all the undergrads and grads  in history of art  school of art  african american studies african studies  and  i think that was  that was those were the departments that  i targeted and all and also to the  galleries gallery guides and gallery  teachers  and i got maybe about 15 or 20  responses and i interviewed and talked  with people and  um felt that the seven that i selected  really brought a range of perspectives  and experiences and  really showed the interest and  commitment to the project that was  sometime in the  early spring of 2013 and we started to  meet in spring semester  the students don't get any  academic credit for this it's a  completely extracurricular  activity for the students it's really  their passion their love their their  interest that drives it  um we broke for the summer which we had  just built up the momentum of forming  the group and then we broke for the for  the summer break and then we got back  together and had to pick up that  momentum  in fall of 2013  um and maybe they can talk about how the  group actually came together how did  i selected you but you you formed a  group  we have some more group performing to do  no we're we're it's been such a pleasure  to work in this group but it did it did  require a lot of um getting comfortable  with one another in a way that we could  be honest about our our ideas and our  preferences and our priorities for the  exhibition and that we could reconcile  our different disciplinary backgrounds  and and wield those differences in the  interest of a very productive uh and and  complete effort um the summer break was  was difficult but um it was okay because  we met and we did site visits in new  york and we saw artwork in galleries and  museums and then we corresponded the  entire time by by email and sometimes by  phone i think one of the other  challenges that now that we're talking  about logistics that came up was  actually fitting in meeting times to our  very busy course schedules i mean  because we're seven students you can  imagine we don't all take classes at the  same time um so we had to to meet um to  schedule meetings whenever we could find  that spare time um so that was an  additional logistical challenge uh but  we i think we came beyond it  yeah i think we would definitely  advocate for you know the the use of  digital technology certainly um i live  in new york and oftentimes it was a  